{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790962350,"nonce":"yPCH0EDSwwArnMjs4Xr7tpw4USM=","title":"Native memory behind a handle is invisible to the GC","message":"A handle is a 16-byte finalizer block; the Rust value behind it can be far\nlarger (a LayoutTree with its taffy tree). Neither collector counts that\nmemory, so it is freed only when an unrelated allocation triggers a\ncollection.\n\nMeasured with 50k `new LayoutTree().createNode()` per round and hl.Gc.major\nbetween rounds:\n- stock HashLink 1.15 (nuc): RSS plateaus at 102 MB from round 2.\n- ash jit and interp (mac, ~/.ash/bin built 2026-09-28): plateaus around\n  1.0-1.06 GB; one round alone peaks at 779 MB. Ash also keeps dead\n  finalizer blocks one extra cycle before running them.\nNothing leaks; trees are reclaimed on both.\n\nA program normally holds one tree, so this matters only for large natives\ncreated and dropped in bulk. Option: an explicit dispose() that drops the\nRust value at once and leaves the finalizer nothing to do.","files":null}]}